# Key ceremony: repository root (YubiKey A) and release key (YubiKey B)

> **Status: OWNER DRAFT, revision 5, 2026-11-06.** Reviewed by Codex three times (`docs/reviews/ceremony/`:
> `CODEX-REVIEW.md` C01–C07, `CODEX-CONFIRM.md` F01–F05, `CODEX-CONFIRM-2.md`, `CODEX-CONFIRM-3.md`). **Not ready for the real
> ceremony until rehearsal 2 validates the raw touch readback in S8** (see "Rehearsal 2" at the end).
> Basis: `docs/V1-RELEASE-KEY-CUSTODY-DRAFT.md` §5–§8 and the first rehearsal (`2026-11-01-rehearsal-1.md`). Key
> type from `docs/V1-PROFILE.md` §15.4: v4 ECDSA P-256. The rehearsal showed a key made this way, on the author's
> Tails stick and a YubiKey 5, signs in a form the verifier's git path accepts, and that touch was enforced.
> It generates the keys only. Using B for a production release needs the custody draft's §9 (independent review,
> exact-hash approval, controlled input media) implemented and rehearsed first; generating B does not close
> A10.4.2's independence requirement (C07).
> **Secret** values (PINs, passphrases, secret keys, backup locations) never go on paper you photograph, into chat,
> the repository or the NUC. **Non-secret** choices and results (versions, serials, fingerprints, pass/fail) go into
> the custody record in the repository.

> **Operator form (2026-11-06):** the author runs `docs/ceremony/OPERATOR-SHEET.md`, one linear process used three
> times (rehearsal on C, root on A, release on B), each with its own two sticks: an encrypted backup (`L-PRIV`) and
> a public stick (`L-PUB`). The structure is the author's suggestion. **Owner decision, same date: one backup per key,
> not two.** Each key then exists as two copies in two places (the YubiKey and the backup stick, stored apart),
> which is adequate for V1: losing both is recoverable (root: a new verifier release; release key: a new release
> lineage), and the second backup's extra stick and steps cost more operator error than they buy. The rehearsal
> uses its own sticks. Where this document and the operator sheet differ in a command, the operator sheet is current.

> **Scripted form (2026-11-06, author's suggestion):** the steps run from `tessera-ceremony.sh`, carried on the INPUT
> stick, checked against its SHA-256 and read by the author before use. Hand-typed commands were error-prone (a
> four-line wrapped command merged visually with the next). **What the checksum does and does not do:** it proves
> the file is the reviewed version; it does not protect against a compromised NUC, which produces both the script
> and its hash. Neither does hand typing, since the printed commands also come from the NUC. The defence in both
> cases is that the author reads what runs. The one risk a script adds, copying the secret onto the public stick, is
> checked by two hand-typed commands at the end of each run (step 11). The INPUT stick is NUC-written and read-only
> in the session; hostile USB firmware is an accepted residual for V1.

## Why two keys, two sessions, separate backups

- **Repository root (A)** signs key-assertion commits and the end-of-issuance statement: the git channel's authority.
- **Release key (B)** signs verifier releases, which carry the trust file naming A. A relying party that installs a
  release authenticated by B trusts the repository keys it lists. So B must not be capturable together with A, the
  DNS credential or the AWS account (A10.4.2, registered).
- So each key gets its own session, passphrase, PINs and backup sticks, and A's and B's backups, papers and tokens are
  kept apart. Then finding one place yields at most one key's material. This does not defend shared hardware or boot
  firmware that is itself compromised (custody draft's peripheral assumption).

## What you need (per key)

| Item | Count | Notes |
|---|---|---|
| Tails boot stick | 1 (shared) | **Persistent Storage must not be unlocked** in these sessions. Boot only. |
| YubiKey | 1 | A or B, labelled. C and D stay spares. |
| Backup sticks | 2 | New, never in the NUC. `ROOT-BK1`/`ROOT-BK2` or `REL-BK1`/`REL-BK2`. |
| Out stick | 1 | New. Public files only. Goes to the NUC afterwards and never returns. |
| Paper | 2 sheets | Public sheet (versions, serial, fingerprint, hashes, pass/fail). Secret sheet (passphrase, PINs), kept apart from the backups. |

Total new sticks for both keys: **6**.

## Owner decisions (2026-11-02; recorded, not secret)

- **Hardware mode**: the key lives on the YubiKey; the two backups are the only other copies, and they are
  exportable signing authority, protected only by their passphrase and where they are kept.
- **Backups**: the passphrase-protected secret-key export on a LUKS2 volume made by Tails' *Disks*. One passphrase
  per key serves both layers; this is one barrier, not two, and is accepted. A's and B's passphrases are independent.
  The actual LUKS parameters are read back and recorded (step S5.4).
- **Card KDF off.** It would keep the PIN off the USB wire. Accepted boundary: the host and its USB path are trusted
  for the session. KDF would not help against a hostile host, which sees the PIN anyway.
- **No expiry** on either key. Validity comes from the trust configuration's signing interval and release lineage.
- **Touch fixed (`uif … permanent`) and signature PIN forced.** If either cannot be confirmed, stop (step S8).
### 15.4 Git evidence [OWNER 2026-10-14, G25]

OWNER CHOICE (supported-path limit) plus SHA-1DC (BROADEN).
- **Commit body** (without `commit <len>\0`): `tree <40 lhex>LF`, `(parent <40 lhex>LF)*`, `author <bytes>LF`,
  `committer <bytes>LF`, exactly one `gpgsig <line>LF` with `( <line>LF)*` continuations, `LF`, message. Repeated
  `parent` headers with distinct ids are allowed (merges); a repeated identical id, any other header (`encoding`,
  `mergetag`, `gpgsig-sha256`, unknown), or a second `tree`, `author`, `committer` or `gpgsig` gives
  `INVALID / GIT_COMMIT_FORMAT`. Author and committer are not interpreted. The signed payload is the commit without
  the `gpgsig` line and its continuations (gitformat-signature(5)).
  - **Empty identity values.** The `<bytes>` of `author` and of `committer` are nonempty: `author` or `committer`
    followed by an empty value is `INVALID / GIT_COMMIT_FORMAT`. [OWNER 2026-10-14, codified from diff-1, A09]
- **Signature value** (continuations joined by LF, one leading space stripped from each): `-----BEGIN PGP
  SIGNATURE-----` LF, one empty line, one or more base64 lines of 1–76 RFC 4648 characters (`=` padding only at the
  end of the last), an optional CRC line `=` plus 4 base64 characters, `-----END PGP SIGNATURE-----`, no trailing LF.
  No armor headers, no other blank lines, no whitespace inside lines. A present CRC-24 must match. Dearmored bytes
  satisfy rule O's grammar and policy (section 8) with type 0x00. Otherwise `INVALID / GIT_SIG_FORMAT`.
  The concatenated base64 lines obey strict RFC 4648 round-trip and padding (no nonzero pad bits), as in section
  15.1; CRC presence and validity remain as above. Every armor, codec and policy violation is `GIT_SIG_FORMAT`, the
  stated exception to section 8's `SIG_POLICY`; failed mathematics is `GIT_SIGNER_NOT_CONFIGURED` (section 6.3).
  [OWNER 2026-10-14, r2-F10, r2-F11]
- **Trees.** Entries `mode SP name NUL id20`; modes {40000, 100644, 100755, 120000, 160000} without leading zero;
  names nonempty, no `/` or NUL, not `.` or `..`; strictly sorted in git order (trees compare as `name/`), no
  duplicates, body consumed exactly. Otherwise `INVALID / GIT_TREE_FORMAT`.
- **Path.** Root entry `tessera-v1` (40000), then `key-assertion` (100644), the blob; `git.trees` is exactly
  [root, sub]. Otherwise `INVALID / GIT_PATH`.
  - A commit whose `tree` header names an id other than the SHA-1 id of `git.trees[0]` is `INVALID / GIT_PATH`
    (row 14.5); there is no dedicated code. [OWNER 2026-10-14, codified from diff-1, EA-22]
- **Ids.** SHA-1 of `"<type> <len>\0" ‖ body` with collision detection (SHA-1DC); a detection is
  `INVALID / GIT_SHA1_COLLISION`. SHA-1DC is a pinned detection implementation and version named in the dependency
  manifest, with its prescribed collision-return behaviour, not whatever a library calls SHA-1 (section 14.2 item
  15). [OWNER 2026-10-14, r2-F14]
- **Repository key.** v4 ECDSA P-256 public-key body, rule O (signer outcomes in section 6.3).
- **Not checked:** that the commit introduced the blob.

### 15.5 Trust configuration [OWNER 2026-10-14, G08, G09, G22]

One RFC 8785 JSON object, no numbers, every key required; authenticated only by the release allowlist (section 5).

**Closure and grammar** [OWNER 2026-10-14, r2-F14]. The schema is closed recursively: every object at every level
(top level, KSK entries, repository entries, `sources` items) has exactly the listed keys. Parsing is strict UTF-8
and JCS with no JSON numbers; binary values are canonical base64 (section 15.1); times use the `utc` grammar of
section 10; decimal strings are canonical unsigned decimals (`0|[1-9][0-9]*`) with per-field ranges: `version` 1 to
4294967295; `key_tag` 0 to 65535; `algorithm` 0 to 255; `reliance_lifetime_s` 1 to 2592000 (**owner choice**,
fail-closed: at most 30 days; the observed value is 1814400); heights as in section 15.1. `root_ksks` is sorted by
(`signing_start`, numeric `key_tag`, `dnskey_rdata` bytes); `repository_keys` by (`signing_start`, `key` bytes).
`root_ksks`, `repository_keys` and every `sources` array are nonempty, and every key in them valid (section 8). An
entry with `signing_end` null must have `signing_start` ≤ `as_of`. A schema failure is a startup refusal
(`TRUST_CONFIG_UNAUTHENTICATED`, no verdict), never a bundle `INVALID`. Cross-release invariants stay in the
release checker; the offline verifier never fetches a previous config. *(Dated correction 2026-10-14: "every key
required" did not say closed at every level, and the sort and decimal rules were less concrete than section 15.1.)*

| Key | Type and grammar | Invariants |
|---|---|---|
| `format` | `"tessera-v1-trust/1"` | |
| `profile` | `"tessera-v1-strict"` | |
| `version` | decimal string ≥ 1 | prior + 1 |
| `previous_sha256` | 64 lowercase hex | 64 zeros for version 1; else the prior config's hash |
| `as_of` | RFC 3339 `YYYY-MM-DDTHH:MM:SSZ` | nondecreasing |
| `issuer_domain` | `"wamason.com"` | equals D |
| `header_pins` | object height-string → 64 lowercase hex (display order) | nonempty; one per height; immutable |
| `root_ksks` | array of KSK entries, sorted by (`signing_start`, `key_tag`) | unique `dnskey_rdata` |
| `repository_keys` | array of `{"domain","key","signing_end","signing_start","sources"}` | `domain` = D; `key` base64 v4 P-256 body; `signing_end` nullable; start ≤ end; unique `key` |

KSK entry (strings unless marked):

| Field | Type | Invariant |
|---|---|---|
| `name` | e.g. `"KSK-2017"` | unique |
| `key_tag` | decimal string 0–65535 | equals RFC 4034 App. B tag of `dnskey_rdata` |
| `algorithm` | decimal string | equals the RDATA algorithm; in {8, 13} |
| `dnskey_rdata` | base64 of RFC 4034 §2.2 RDATA | flags 257, REVOKE clear, protocol 3 |
| `ds_sha256` | 64 lowercase hex | SHA-256 over root owner wire `00` ‖ RDATA |
| `signing_start`, `signing_end` | RFC 3339; `signing_end` nullable | start ≤ end |
# Operator-sheet findings — 2026-11-06

Line numbers refer to `docs/ceremony/OPERATOR-SHEET.md` (SHA-256 `0fa7d137651e500867f7e3ef03eb78376a43671557dbd64ef49dc5d1c6f241e7`). Document and GnuPG 2.4 source review; no Tails/card rehearsal performed.

- **HIGH — lines 17, 36–37, 77, 89, 152–153: the blanket shutdown/restart rule is unsafe and contradicts the recovery instructions.** If step 6's export or restore fails while the new key exists only in RAM, line 17 discards that working copy instead of repairing the backup in the same session as line 77 requires. If the backup becomes unreadable after `keytocard`, a later Part 1 error followed by shutdown and rehearsal C's reset destroys the remaining working card copy. For A/B, restarting instead reaches step 2's “no keys” stop; it does not reset the card as line 17 promises. Replace the blanket rule with phase-specific recovery: before a proven backup, retain the live keyring and repair the backup; after transfer, preserve the card and backup and seek an owner decision. Explicitly state which instruction wins when errors occur.

- **MEDIUM — lines 37, 100–105: unconditional `forcesig` can turn the required policy off.** An empty A/B card with retries `3 0 3` can already have forced signature PIN enabled; step 2 does not constrain that setting. Running `forcesig` once then disables it, and step 9 stops after key transfer. Read the setting first and toggle only when it is `not forced`, then retain the existing readback requirement. GnuPG implements this command as a [toggle of the current CHV status](https://github.com/gpg/gnupg/blob/gnupg-2.4.7/g10/card-util.c#L1205-L1224).

- **MEDIUM — lines 114–116, 152: `NO-SIGNATURE-OK` does not establish a touch timeout.** Canceling PIN entry, entering a wrong PIN, or losing the card connection also leaves no signature and prints the accepted marker. Thus a positive test followed by a canceled negative test can satisfy the written conditions without testing refusal for lack of touch. Require the expected touch-timeout diagnostic and a nonzero signing exit status, after successful PIN entry with the card still present; treat other errors as inconclusive and rerun after recovery. Preserve the no-output-file check as an additional condition.
